<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">War Guilt Clause</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Article 231</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War Guilt Clause, Article 231 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1919, required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to accept responsibility for the loss and damage caused by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. It provided the legal basis for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> note its impact, since resentment of the clause shaped German politics in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> and beyond.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-guilt-clause.jpg" alt="War Guilt Clause" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noël Dorville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_Peace_Conference.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
